WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.190
Please welcome Matthew Dicks.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:10.170
I'm sitting in a straight back chair
and I'm watching the greatest magic

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.300
show I have ever seen in my life.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:17.850
The name of the Magicians are Clara
and Charlie, ages seven and three.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.725
They have been preparing this
performance for more than an hour.

00:00:22.725 --> 00:00:26.760
There are moving furniture and
gathering props and making costumes

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.560
and rehearsing relentlessly.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.799
There's a table in the middle of the
room and they've put a blanket over

00:00:31.799 --> 00:00:36.480
it and on top of the blanket there's
coins and cups and rubber balls

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.570
and an inflatable sort of rabbit.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.500
And Charlie is walking around with a
cape made of construction paper that

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.640
Clara has taped all over his back
and they open the magic show with

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.349
Charlie saying, ladies and gentlemen,
just over and over and over again.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.050
And it's amazing.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.239
It's like a fantastic magic show.

00:00:58.019 --> 00:00:59.400
The magic is atrocious.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.940
They're like the worst, the worst
magicians you've ever seen in your life.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.610
Like every trick is worse than the last.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.610
There's, there's the disappearing
Charlie trick where Clara takes the

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.910
cape and she holds it in front of
Charlie and she says, abracadabra.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:15.870
And Charlie runs and hides behind
the couch, but he doesn't duck.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.870
So he's just standing there
laughing when the Cape falls.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.670
And they're eating apples during
the entire performance, like big

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.390
apples and little hands and every,
like, everything's sticky and

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:31.080
like, it's so like decidedly less
professional than it really should be.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.830
But the worst trick is the
disappearing dime trick.

00:01:35.414 --> 00:01:36.464
Clara holds her hand out.

00:01:36.464 --> 00:01:39.104
She's got a dime in it, and she
says, I'm going to make this vanish.

00:01:39.225 --> 00:01:43.664
And she quenches her hand into a fist
and then tells us to close our eyes.

00:01:45.074 --> 00:01:46.065
And so we do.

00:01:46.304 --> 00:01:48.554
And I'm wondering what she's
gonna do with the dime.

00:01:48.554 --> 00:01:52.274
And it takes a second before I realize
because she's taking the dime and she's

00:01:52.274 --> 00:01:57.645
jamming it down my ear canal further than
anything has ever been down my ear before.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.070
And after a minute she's,
she says, open your eyes.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:05.429
And she's got the fist and she
opens it and the dime is gone.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:09.780
And my wife, who is unaware of the assault
and battery that has just taken place to

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.750
the right of her, says, where's the dime?

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.230
And Clara reaches behind my ear
like a magician would to pluck it.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:20.820
But instead, she digs deep with
her index finger and gets it out.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.560
It's horrible and it's
beautiful at the same time.

00:02:26.655 --> 00:02:29.894
And I look at my wife, Alicia, and
she's laughing and she has tears in

00:02:29.894 --> 00:02:32.535
her eyes, and I know why she has tears.

00:02:33.015 --> 00:02:36.584
It's because she's in the moment, like
she's in the now and she's watching these

00:02:36.584 --> 00:02:38.565
two brilliant children do this thing.

00:02:39.315 --> 00:02:41.174
And I am never in the moment.

00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:44.924
I am a man like trapped
between two worlds.

00:02:45.255 --> 00:02:51.495
I am forever looking to a future that
I do not like or want, and at the

00:02:51.495 --> 00:02:53.685
same time, I'm anchored to a past.

00:02:54.885 --> 00:02:55.935
I can't escape.

00:02:57.165 --> 00:02:59.355
When I look at my kids
do things like this.

00:02:59.355 --> 00:03:03.704
I cannot help but see these dark
shadows on the edges and I can't

00:03:03.704 --> 00:03:07.755
help but literally think as I watch
them, that someday this family of

00:03:07.755 --> 00:03:13.935
four is gonna be broken and one by
one, each one of us is going to die.

00:03:13.935 --> 00:03:19.424
And this unforgettable moment that we're
having will one day be forgotten forever.

00:03:20.625 --> 00:03:25.725
And I know that that is dark, like
that is as dark as dark can be.

00:03:26.445 --> 00:03:30.195
And it's true, but it's, it's
not completely black for me.

00:03:30.195 --> 00:03:33.555
There's a little bit of hope, there's a
light because there's tears in my eyes

00:03:33.555 --> 00:03:36.285
too and they're not tears for the now.

00:03:36.285 --> 00:03:41.535
There are tears from a past, a past
that has made me who I am, and it's a

00:03:41.535 --> 00:03:43.170
past that gives me hope for the future.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:48.960
In the summer of 1993, I was 22
years old and I was feeling great.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.970
My life was finally getting started.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.870
After high school, I had a lot of
struggles, but I was finally getting

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.500
ready to go to college, and that
meant I might have a chance in

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.420
making my childhood dreams come true.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.250
And they were two and they were simple.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:06.900
I wanted to be a school teacher and
I wanted to be a writer, and I fought

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.450
like hell to get to that point.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:15.090
And then $7,000 went missing at a
McDonald's where I was managing.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.870
And although my boss didn't think I
had stolen the money and my boss's

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:23.580
boss didn't think I had taken it, we
ran into a police officer who did.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.330
And very quickly I was
arrested for something I didn't

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.190
do, and I was put in jail.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.849
And when I was arraigned in front
of the judge, I explained to the

00:04:32.849 --> 00:04:35.219
judge that I was gonna lose my job.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.690
So I wasn't going to have any money.

00:04:37.365 --> 00:04:40.995
And I was gonna lose my home because
my friend was moving and I was

00:04:40.995 --> 00:04:44.055
supposed to be going to the dorms
and that wasn't going to happen

00:04:44.055 --> 00:04:47.565
now, and I would end up homeless.

00:04:47.835 --> 00:04:48.945
And I didn't have a family.

00:04:48.945 --> 00:04:52.455
I told 'em, I didn't have parents to
support me or anyone to take care of me.

00:04:53.205 --> 00:04:58.875
But the judge told me that I couldn't have
a court appointed attorney because my car,

00:04:59.115 --> 00:05:03.255
a 1992 Toyota Tercel, was nicer than his.

00:05:03.675 --> 00:05:04.395
His words.

00:05:05.385 --> 00:05:10.575
And so that meant I was gonna have
a $25,000 legal fee, which might as

00:05:10.575 --> 00:05:12.645
well have been $25 million to me.

00:05:13.395 --> 00:05:16.005
And all the things I told the
judge, what happened, happened.

00:05:16.034 --> 00:05:17.775
I got fired from my job.

00:05:18.104 --> 00:05:19.155
I lost my home.

00:05:19.575 --> 00:05:23.385
I lived in my car for weeks and
then I caught a lucky break.

00:05:23.805 --> 00:05:26.745
Friends, people who used to work
with me at another McDonald's, Mary

00:05:26.745 --> 00:05:28.245
and Jerry, born again Christians.

00:05:28.755 --> 00:05:31.905
They took me in, they found out I was
homeless and said, come live with us.

00:05:31.905 --> 00:05:34.275
And I, I lived in a
room off their kitchen.

00:05:34.695 --> 00:05:38.594
I shared it with a guy named Rick
who spoke in tongues in his sleep,

00:05:39.284 --> 00:05:42.044
and Mary and Jerry's indoor pet goat.

00:05:42.945 --> 00:05:46.094
It was actually the goat's room
before Rick and I came along.

00:05:46.094 --> 00:05:47.715
So we couldn't complain really.

00:05:48.825 --> 00:05:50.174
And when I got there it was okay.

00:05:50.174 --> 00:05:51.075
'cause then I had a roof.

00:05:51.075 --> 00:05:53.294
And when you have a roof, you
have an address and a phone,

00:05:53.294 --> 00:05:54.885
and that means you can get work.

00:05:54.885 --> 00:05:57.885
And so I got jobs, I worked
in an office during the day.

00:05:58.229 --> 00:06:02.280
And I went to work at McDonald's at
night because even though I was actually

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:06.599
going to be on trial for stealing money
from McDonald's, this one was owned by

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:10.409
a man, Andy Chung, a franchisee, and
he knew I hadn't taken the money, and

00:06:10.409 --> 00:06:12.210
so he gave me a job closing the store.

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:12.710
At night.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.890
I worked 80 hours a week
for more than 18 months.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:18.510
I barely saw the goat,

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:25.885
and over that time, and over
that time, I was just so angry.

00:06:26.955 --> 00:06:29.265
I was angry at my parents
for not being there.

00:06:29.505 --> 00:06:33.315
I was angry at a police officer and a
judge who had put me in this terrible

00:06:33.315 --> 00:06:38.835
position, but mostly I was just angry
at the world for constantly reigning bad

00:06:38.835 --> 00:06:42.765
luck down upon me at the moment that I
thought I was gonna finally get ahead.

00:06:43.905 --> 00:06:46.965
And then everything changed on April 19th.

00:06:46.965 --> 00:06:49.605
It was midnight and I was in McDonald's.

00:06:49.665 --> 00:06:53.325
I was closing the store, the doors were
locked, my crew were cleaning up, and

00:06:53.325 --> 00:06:54.560
I was counting the money at the safe.

00:06:55.635 --> 00:07:00.015
I had another $7,000 in my hand and
I was stuffing it into a deposit bag

00:07:00.015 --> 00:07:01.245
so it could be brought to the bank.

00:07:01.245 --> 00:07:06.525
The next morning when I heard the crash
and I knew what it was, the police had

00:07:06.525 --> 00:07:10.425
come a week before to warn me about the
three men who were robbing restaurants

00:07:10.425 --> 00:07:12.465
in the town, and I knew about it.

00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:15.765
My girlfriends McDonald's on the other
side of town had already been robbed.

00:07:16.185 --> 00:07:18.914
And the Taco Bell that I could
see outside my drive through

00:07:18.914 --> 00:07:20.534
window had been robbed a week ago.

00:07:20.534 --> 00:07:22.724
And in that robbery,
they had killed someone.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:28.200
So when I heard the glass break,
I knew who was coming for me and I

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.330
made the worst decision of my life.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.830
I took that bag full of that money and
I reached into the back of the safe,

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:39.090
and I dropped it down the slot that
sent it to a compartment at the bottom

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.215
of the safe that I couldn't open.

00:07:41.985 --> 00:07:45.015
And then they were in the room with me
and they had brought my crew members,

00:07:45.015 --> 00:07:48.675
two Haitian brothers who couldn't speak
any English, and a girl named Francis,

00:07:48.675 --> 00:07:52.335
an 18-year-old girl who I would take to
the prom a month later because her mother

00:07:52.335 --> 00:07:54.015
didn't trust her with anyone but me.

00:07:54.615 --> 00:07:58.425
And they had us all lie down
on this greasy red tile floor.

00:07:58.695 --> 00:08:04.724
Three men in black shirts and black pants
and white sneakers and big black guns.

00:08:05.325 --> 00:08:07.905
And they started to open the
safe and take out the money.

00:08:08.145 --> 00:08:10.484
And they realized very quickly
that there wasn't enough money

00:08:10.484 --> 00:08:11.625
here, that something was wrong.

00:08:11.625 --> 00:08:14.265
And so they grabbed me and
picked me up and they told me

00:08:14.265 --> 00:08:15.765
to open that bottom compartment.

00:08:16.275 --> 00:08:17.505
And I told them I couldn't.

00:08:17.775 --> 00:08:22.275
There's a, there was a plaque and I said,
manager, can't open owner has a key.

00:08:22.275 --> 00:08:23.474
And they didn't believe me.

00:08:23.864 --> 00:08:26.625
And so they started to beat me
with the guns and they started to

00:08:26.625 --> 00:08:28.905
kick me and I pleaded with them.

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:31.830
That they would believe me
that I couldn't open it.

00:08:32.429 --> 00:08:37.590
And then I felt a gun pushed up against
my head, and I heard one of them

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:42.419
say that, I'm gonna count back from
three and then I'm gonna shoot you in

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:44.970
the head if you don't open that box.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:50.250
And as they counted back from three,
I begged this man to believe me that I

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:56.939
could not get the money as he counted
3, 2, 1, and then there was a click.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.569
It was the loudest click in
the history of the world.

00:09:02.339 --> 00:09:06.120
And then they laughed and I cried
and they threw me to the ground and I

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:10.349
tried to crawl away 'cause I couldn't
talk anymore and I couldn't breathe.

00:09:10.829 --> 00:09:13.859
And they pulled me back
and they hit me more.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:19.949
And then I felt a gun press into my head
and push my face down onto that tile.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.584
And he said, this time there's a bullet.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:28.230
And I'm gonna shoot you if you don't
open that box for McDonald's money.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:29.970
So open the safe.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:37.770
And as he started to count back from
three, I felt all the fear and anger in me

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:42.600
just roll over to sadness and to regret.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:49.080
I was a 22-year-old kid on a greasy
tile floor and I was going to die

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.570
having done nothing with my life.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.600
And I would be forgotten by everyone.

00:09:56.670 --> 00:10:00.689
And when he hit zero, there was
another click and they laughed

00:10:00.689 --> 00:10:02.700
some more and I just wept.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:05.430
And then they were gone.

00:10:07.439 --> 00:10:13.319
And for the next 15 years, I suffered from
post-traumatic stress disorder until I met

00:10:13.319 --> 00:10:17.610
my wife and she told me that I probably
shouldn't wake up every night screaming.

00:10:17.939 --> 00:10:18.839
And I said, no.

00:10:18.839 --> 00:10:23.490
It's like my thing, like some people
collect stamps and some people

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:25.469
play tennis and this is what I do.

00:10:26.819 --> 00:10:29.370
And she said, no, that's
not actually a thing.

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:33.240
And she sent me to someone
and I got a little better.

00:10:34.425 --> 00:10:39.255
And six months after the robbery, I was
found not guilty in a court of law, and

00:10:39.255 --> 00:10:42.255
I restarted my life and I was relentless.

00:10:42.584 --> 00:10:44.025
I paid off my debt.

00:10:44.265 --> 00:10:45.915
I got myself back to college.

00:10:45.915 --> 00:10:50.265
And in 1999 I graduated and I
became an elementary school teacher.

00:10:50.535 --> 00:10:53.775
And for the last 18 years,
hundreds of kids have passed

00:10:53.775 --> 00:10:54.915
through my classroom door.

00:10:55.605 --> 00:10:59.745
And in 2009, I published my
first novel, something Missing,

00:11:00.135 --> 00:11:01.514
and I've published 3 cent.

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:09.000
A long time ago, there was a boy
lying on a greasy tile floor thinking

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:12.359
that his life was gonna be over and
he will have accomplished nothing.

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:17.310
And somehow I managed to make
those childhood dreams come true.

00:11:18.959 --> 00:11:27.839
And yet so much of me now, like all of me
now, is still lying on that tile floor.

00:11:30.074 --> 00:11:36.915
No matter how much success I've had in
my life, I live with a constant ringing

00:11:36.915 --> 00:11:45.045
fear that there will be a day not too
far in the future when I will be in

00:11:45.045 --> 00:11:51.495
those last moments of my life, and
that I will feel the same regret that

00:11:51.495 --> 00:11:54.464
I felt that day in that McDonald's.

00:11:55.665 --> 00:11:57.285
And so when I wake up every morning.

00:11:57.975 --> 00:12:04.454
I wake up in fear that I will
not fill this day with enough

00:12:05.235 --> 00:12:07.064
to make that regret go away.

00:12:08.895 --> 00:12:13.785
I'm a, I've spent my life
chasing a horizon that I cannot

00:12:13.785 --> 00:12:17.415
catch, and so that is what I do.

00:12:17.985 --> 00:12:25.515
I fill every day that I have with writing
and with teaching, and with beautiful.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.320
Terrible magic shows

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:36.570
and I hope and I pray that
someday it will be enough.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:37.850
Thank you.

